    description: "**Found via self-analysis dogfooding on 2026-05-16 (round 11).**\n\n**Symptom.** `hypergumbo dead-code-maybe` lists many shell-function names from scripts as \"potentially dead\" with a literal `?` shown in the LOC column instead of a numeric line count.\n\nSample from self-analysis output:\n\n```\npass                           scripts/release-check              ? LOC\nhave                           .githooks/commit-msg               ? LOC\nhave                           scripts/install-hooks              ? LOC\nwarn                           scripts/ci-failover                ? LOC\nwarn                           scripts/release-check              ? LOC\nfail                           scripts/release-check              ? LOC\ninstall                        packages/htrac-frontend/public/service-worker.js     ? LOC\nusage                          scripts/ci-debug                   ? LOC\nusage                          scripts/list-my-prs                ? LOC\nusage                          scripts/merge-pr                   ? LOC\nusage                          scripts/stop-hook-preview          ? LOC\nconnect                        packages/htrac-frontend/src/ws-client.ts     ? LOC\nclose                          packages/htrac-frontend/src/ws-client.ts     ? LOC\nsection                        scripts/release-check              ? LOC\n```\n\n**Two problems here.**\n\n**Problem 1: `? LOC` is a formatting fallback indicating `lines_of_code` is null.**\n\nSpot-check on the self-analysis baseline confirmed (cf. R11-2 baseline node printout): bash-language Symbols have `lines_of_code: None`. The dead-code formatter falls back to `?` when LOC is null. So bash analyzer is emitting Symbols without computing a LOC count for them.\n\nPython and TypeScript Symbols have numeric LOC, so the formatter shows e.g. `Cache.delete ... 8 LOC`. Bash is the language that consistently shows `?`.\n\n**Problem 2: Most of these shell functions are likely false positives.**\n\nA function named `have`, `pass`, `warn`, `fail`, `usage`, `section` etc. in a shell script is overwhelmingly likely to be a helper function called from elsewhere in the same script. The bash analyzer probably doesn't capture intra-script function calls, so these helpers appear with zero in-edges → flagged dead.\n\nVerification (manual): `scripts/release-check` has functions `pass`, `warn`, `fail`, `section`. A quick `grep -nE \"^\\s*(pass|warn|fail|section)\\b\" scripts/release-check` will confirm these are called many times within the script. The bash analyzer just isn't seeing those calls.\n\n**Why this matters.**\n\n1. **Noise in dead-code output.** Combined with R11-3 (polymorphic dispatch FPs), the `dead-code-maybe` output is dominated by FPs from at least two distinct mechanisms.\n\n2. **The `? LOC` formatting tells the user \"we don't even know how big this function is.\"** That's a signal that the bash analyzer is incomplete in basic Symbol metadata, not just call-graph coverage.\n\n3. **Scope analogue to INV-mofav.** INV-mofav (round 10) identified that the Python analyzer drops nested-function definitions and bare-name calls to them. This bash variant is similar in shape: the analyzer is missing call edges that the source clearly contains. Different language, related completeness gap.\n\n**Severity.** Low-medium. The dead-code-maybe output for shell scripts is essentially noise. Limited blast radius (only affects bash output), but it does prevent the tool from being useful on shell-heavy repos (CI scripts, DevOps tooling, etc.).\n\n**Fix proposal — three parts.**\n\n1. **Bash analyzer should emit `lines_of_code` for every function Symbol it creates.** Compute as `end_line - start_line + 1` or count non-empty lines per the existing convention (WI-sosog filed round 8). Cosmetic fix, prevents the `?` fallback.\n\n2. **Bash analyzer should capture intra-script function calls.** A function-name token at the start of a non-keyword line is a call. The implementation needs to:\n   - Know which names are defined as functions in the same script (already does, since they're emitted as Symbols)\n   - On each \"command\" line, check if the first token matches a known function name\n   - Emit a `calls` edge from the enclosing function (or module if at top level) to the called function\n\n3. **Optional: cross-script call detection** via `source` / `.` invocations is harder; defer if (2) catches most cases.\n\n**Test gap.** Use `scripts/release-check` as a fixture: assert that the `pass`, `warn`, `fail`, `section` functions in that script all have non-zero in-degree on call edges. Currently they have zero.\n\n**Cross-references.**\n\n- `packages/hypergumbo-lang-mainstream/src/hypergumbo_lang_mainstream/bash.py` (or wherever the bash analyzer lives — needs confirmation)\n- `INV-mofav-...` (filed round 10) — Python analyzer's nested-function blindness; same completeness gap pattern in a different language\n- `INV-hokig-...` (filed round 9) — profile/analyzer disagree on bash file count (10 vs 41); sibling bash-coverage issue\n- `WI-sosog-...` (filed round 8) — LOC docs gap; if `_count_loc` is used by the bash analyzer the docs should note bash special-case"  # fac9
    fields: {}  # fac9
